messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B or C.\n####\n# Context:\n"Stranger Things" is a popular Netflix show. If a Netflix show is popular, Karen will binge-watch it. If and only if Karen binge-watches a Netflix show, she will download it. Karen does not download "Black Mirror". "Black Mirror" is a Netflix show. If Karen binge-watches a Netflix show, she will share it to Lisa.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or uncertain? Karen will share "Black Mirror" to Lisa.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Uncertain', '<think>.\nOkay, so I\'m trying to figure out whether the statement "Karen will share \'Black Mirror\' to Lisa" is true, false, or uncertain based on the given context. Let me break down the information step by step.\n\nFirst, the context tells me that "Stranger Things" is a popular Netflix show. Then it says that if a Netflix show is popular, Karen will binge-watch it. So, since "Stranger Things" is popular, Karen will binge-watch it. That makes sense.\n\nNext, it mentions that if and only if Karen binge-watches a Netflix show, she will download it. So, this is an "if and only if" situation, which means it\'s a biconditional statement. That implies that both directions are true: if she downloads a show, she must have binge-watched it, and if she binge-watches it, she will download it. So, it\'s a two-way implication.\n\nThen, it says that Karen does not download "Black Mirror." So, she doesn\'t have it on her device. Now, "Black Mirror" is a Netflix show. The question is whether she will share it to Lisa. .\n\nLet me outline the connections:.\n\n1. "Stranger Things" is popular → Karen will binge-watch it.\n2. If Karen binge-watches a show, she will download it.\n3. Karen does not download "Black Mirror.".\n4. "Black Mirror" is a Netflix show.\n5. If Karen binge-watches a show, she will share it to Lisa.\n\nWait, but the context doesn\'t mention whether Karen watches "Stranger Things" or not. It just says that because it\'s popular, she will watch it. So, she will watch "Stranger Things," which means she will download it. But what about "Black Mirror"? The context doesn\'t say she watches it. It only mentions that she doesn\'t download it. .\n\nSo, the key points are:.\n\n- Karen doesn\'t download "Black Mirror.".\n- She only downloads shows she\'s binge-watched.\n- If she binge-watches a show, she shares it with Lisa.\n\nBut since she doesn\'t download "Black Mirror," that means she didn\'t binge-watch it either. So, she didn\'t watch it, so she didn\'t share it. Therefore, she won\'t share "Black Mirror" to Lisa.\n\nWait, but the question is about "Black Mirror." The context says she doesn\'t download it, but it doesn\'t say she doesn\'t watch it. Hmm, but the statement is about downloading, not watching. So, if she doesn\'t download it, she won\'t share it. .\n\nBut wait, the context says that she only downloads shows she\'s binge-watched. So, if she didn\'t download it, she didn\'t watch it. Therefore, she didn\'t watch it, so she didn\'t share it. So, she won\'t share "Black Mirror" to Lisa.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the context doesn\'t specify whether she watches "Stranger Things" or not. It just says she will watch it because it\'s popular. So, she will watch "Stranger Things," download it, and share it. But for "Black Mirror," she doesn\'t download it, so she doesn\'t share it.\n\nSo, the statement is that she will share "Black Mirror" to Lisa. But since she doesn\'t download it, she won\'t share it. Therefore, the statement is false.\n\nWait, but let me make sure. The context says that if she doesn\'t download a show, she won\'t share it. So, since she doesn\'t download "Black Mirror," she won\'t share it. Therefore, the statement is false</think>.\n\nThe statement is false because Karen does not download "Black Mirror," so she won\'t share it with Lisa. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
