messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B or C.\n####\n# Context:\nA person is either a Grand Slam champion or an Oscar-nominated actor. All people who are Grand Slam champions are professional tennis players. All Oscar-nominated actors are celebrities. All professional tennis players are athletes. If a person is a celebrity then they are well paid. If a person is an athlete then they are famous. All well-paid people live in tax havens. If Djokovic is famous and is an athlete, then Djokovic lives in well paid.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or uncertain? Djokovic does not live in a tax haven.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Uncertain', '<think>\nOkay, so I\'m trying to figure out whether the statement "Djokovic does not live in a tax haven" is true, false, or uncertain based on the given context. Let me break down the information step by step.\n\nFirst, the context tells me that a person is either a Grand Slam champion or an Oscar-nominated actor. So, Djokovic must fall into one of these two categories. Let\'s see what each category implies.\n\nAll Grand Slam champions are professional tennis players. So, if Djokovic is a Grand Slam champion, he\'s a professional tennis player. Then, all professional tennis players are athletes. So, if he\'s a professional tennis player, he\'s an athlete. \n\nAlternatively, if Djokovic is an Oscar-nominated actor, then he\'s a celebrity. And all celebrities are well paid. Also, all athletes are famous. So, if he\'s an athlete, he\'s famous. \n\nNow, the next part says that all well-paid people live in tax havens. So, if someone is well paid, they live in a tax haven. Conversely, if someone lives in a tax haven, they are well paid. \n\nThe statement given is that if Djokovic is famous and an athlete, then he lives in a tax haven. Let\'s see what that implies. If he\'s an athlete, he\'s famous. So, being an athlete makes him famous. Then, being famous makes him well paid, because all celebrities are well paid. Being well paid means he lives in a tax haven. So, if he\'s an athlete, he must live in a tax haven.\n\nWait, but the question is about whether he does not live in a tax haven. So, if he is an athlete, he does live in a tax haven. Therefore, the statement that he does not live in a tax haven would be false. But wait, let me make sure.\n\nWait, the context says that if a person is a celebrity, they are well paid, and if they are an athlete, they are famous. But the key point is that all well-paid people live in tax havens. So, being well paid implies living in a tax haven. \n\nNow, if Djokovic is a Grand Slam champion, he\'s a professional tennis player, hence an athlete. Being an athlete makes him famous. Being famous makes him well paid, hence living in a tax haven. So, if he\'s a Grand Slam champion, he lives in a tax haven, which would make the statement "Djokovic does not live in a tax haven" false.\n\nAlternatively, if he\'s an Oscar-nominated actor, he\'s a celebrity, hence well paid, hence living in a tax haven. So, regardless of whether he\'s a Grand Slam champion or an Oscar-nominated actor, he would live in a tax haven.\n\nWait, but the statement says "if Djokovic is famous and is an athlete, then he lives in a tax haven." So, if he\'s famous and an athlete, he lives in a tax haven. But the question is about whether he does not live in a tax haven. So, if he is famous and an athlete, he does live in a tax haven, making the statement false. But if he is not famous or not an athlete, does that affect anything?\n\nWait, but from the context, if he\'s a Grand Slam champion, he\'s an athlete, hence famous, hence well paid, hence in a tax haven. If he\'s an Oscar-nominated actor, he\'s a celebrity, hence well paid, hence in a tax haven. So, in either case, he must live in a tax haven. Therefore, the statement that he does not live in a tax haven would be false.\n\nWait, but the question is phrased as "If Djokovic is famous and is an athlete, then he lives in well paid." So, the conclusion is that he lives in a tax haven. Therefore, the statement "Djokovic does not live in a tax haven" would be false because he does live in a tax haven.\n\nSo, putting it all together, regardless of whether he\'s a Grand Slam champion or an Oscar-nominated actor, he must live in a tax haven. Therefore, the statement that he does not live in a tax haven is false.\n</think>.\n\nThe statement is false because, based on the given premises, Djokovic must live in a tax haven. Whether he is a Grand Slam champion or an Oscar-nominated actor, he ends up living in a tax haven. Therefore, my final option is: ']] 13%|#2        | 26/204 [00:04<00:19,  9.24it/s]
