: All of Max\'s favorite animals love to sleep. So, quokka, being Max\'s favorite, loves to sleep.\n\nNow, point 6: If a koala is very fluffy, then it\'s not a quokka. So, being very fluffy excludes a koala from being a quokka.\n\nBut wait, from point 4, we know that all Max\'s favorite animals are very fluffy. So, quokka is very fluffy, and koalas, if they are very fluffy, can\'t be quokka. But does this mean that koalas are necessarily very fluffy?\n\nLet me think. The context doesn\'t explicitly say whether koalas are very fluffy or not. It only says that if a koala is very fluffy, then it\'s not a quokka. But it doesn\'t provide any information about whether koalas are actually very fluffy or not.\n\nSo, from the given information, we know that quokka is very fluffy and loves to sleep. But for koalas, we don\'t have any direct information about their fluffiness or their sleeping habits. The only connection is that if a koala is very fluffy, it\'s not a quokka. But since quokka is already very fluffy, and koalas can\'t be quokka if they\'re very fluffy, but we don\'t know if koalas are very fluffy or not.\n\nTherefore, the statement "Koalas love to sleep" isn\'t directly supported or contradicted by the given information. We don\'t have any data about koalas\' sleeping habits. So, it\'s uncertain.\n</think>.\n\nThe statement "Koalas love to sleep" is uncertain because the given information doesn\'t provide any direct evidence about koalas\' sleeping habits. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
