messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B or C.\n####\n# Context:\nSusan flies to LGA airport. The departure and arrival can not be the same airport. John flies from LGA airport.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or uncertain? John flies to LGA airport.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Uncertain', '<think>.\nOkay, so I\'m trying to figure out this logic problem. Let me read it again carefully.\n\nThe context says that Susan flies to LGA airport, and it\'s mentioned that the departure and arrival can\'t be the same airport. Then, John flies from LGA airport. The question is whether the statement "John flies to LGA airport" is true, false, or uncertain.\n\nHmm, let\'s break this down. First, Susan flies to LGA. That means her destination is LGA, but her departure airport isn\'t specified. It just says they can\'t be the same, so Susan must have departed from a different airport than LGA.\n\nNow, John flies from LGA. So John\'s departure airport is LGA. The question is about where John is flying to. The statement in question is whether John flies to LGA. .\n\nWait, so John departs from LGA, but where does he arrive? The context doesn\'t say anything about where John is going. It only specifies that Susan\'s departure isn\'t the same as her arrival, but nothing about John\'s destination.\n\nSo, John could be flying to any airport, including LGA or another. Since the context doesn\'t provide information about John\'s destination, we don\'t know for sure whether he\'s flying to LGA or not. Therefore, the statement is uncertain based on the given information</think>.\n\nThe statement is uncertain because the context doesn\'t specify where John is flying to. He could be going to LGA or another airport. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
