all B are S. So, if someone is a B, they must be S. Therefore, if someone is not S, they cannot be a B. So, ¬S(Yuri) implies ¬B(Yuri). Therefore, if Yuri is not wearing large shoes, Yuri is not a basketball player.\n\nBut the statement we\'re evaluating is: If ¬S(Yuri), then (B(Yuri) ∧ A(Yuri)). So, if ¬S(Yuri), then Yuri is a B and an A.\n\nBut from the context, if ¬S(Yuri), then ¬B(Yuri). So, the antecedent of the statement is ¬S(Yuri), which would imply ¬B(Yuri). Therefore, the consequent (B(Yuri) ∧ A(Yuri)) would be false because B(Yuri) is false.\n\nSo, the implication would be: If ¬S(Yuri), then false. In logic, an implication is only false if the antecedent is true and the consequent is false. So, if ¬S(Yuri) is true, then the implication is false. But if ¬S(Yuri) is false, the implication is true regardless of the consequent.\n\nBut wait, the question is whether the statement is true, false, or uncertain. So, we need to see if the statement is necessarily true, necessarily false, or uncertain based on the given information.\n\nLet me think about it again. The statement is: If ¬S(Yuri), then (B(Yuri) ∧ A(Yuri)).\n\nFrom the context, we know that all B are S. Therefore, if Yuri is a B, then S(Yuri) must be true. So, if Yuri is a B, ¬S(Yuri) is false. Therefore, the implication "If ¬S(Yuri), then (B(Yuri) ∧ A(Yuri))" would only be relevant if ¬S(Yuri) is true, which would mean Yuri is not a B.\n\nBut the statement says that if ¬S(Yuri), then Yuri is a B and an A. But if ¬S(Yuri), then Yuri is not a B, as per the context. Therefore, the consequent (B(Yuri) ∧ A(Yuri)) would be false. So, the implication would be "If true, then false," which is false.\n\nWait, but that\'s only if ¬S(Yuri) is true. But the statement is about the implication, not about the truth of ¬S(Yuri). So, the statement is saying that whenever ¬S(Yuri) is true, then (B(Yuri) ∧ A(Yuri)) must be true. But from the context, if ¬S(Yuri) is true, then Yuri is not a B, making (B(Yuri) ∧ A(Yuri)) fal