messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B or C.\n####\n# Context:\nIt costs US $205 to take the GRE test. ETS provides financial aid to those GRE applicants who prove economic hardship. Economic hardship refers to difficulty caused by having too little money or too few resources. Tom lives in a single-parent family. His dad has been out of work for more than a year.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or uncertain? There is no financial aid available for the GRE test.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Uncertain', '<think>\nOkay, so I need to figure out whether the statement "There is no financial aid available for the GRE test." is true, false, or uncertain based on the given context. Let me break this down step by step.\n\nFirst, I\'ll read the context carefully. It says that it costs US $205 to take the GRE test. Then, ETS (the organization that administers the GRE) provides financial aid to those applicants who prove economic hardship. Economic hardship is defined as difficulty caused by having too little money or too few resources. \n\nNow, the context mentions Tom, who lives in a single-parent family. His dad has been out of work for more than a year. So, Tom is facing economic hardship because his father is unemployed, which means Tom doesn\'t have enough money or resources.\n\nThe question is asking about the availability of financial aid for the GRE test. The context explicitly states that ETS offers financial aid to those proving economic hardship. Since Tom is in a situation of economic hardship, he would be eligible for this aid.\n\nTherefore, the statement "There is no financial aid available for the GRE test." is false because the context clearly mentions that financial aid is provided.\n</think>.\n\nThe statement is false because the context indicates that ETS provides financial aid for the GRE test to those in economic hardship, including Tom\'s situation. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
