messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B or C.\n####\n# Context:\n"Stranger Things" is a popular Netflix show. If a Netflix show is popular, Karen will binge-watch it. If and only if Karen binge-watches a Netflix show, she will download it. Karen does not download "Black Mirror". "Black Mirror" is a Netflix show. If Karen binge-watches a Netflix show, she will share it to Lisa.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or uncertain? "Black Mirror" is popular.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Uncertain', '<think>.\nOkay, so I\'m trying to figure out whether the statement "Black Mirror" is popular based on the given context. Let me break down the information step by step.\n\nFirst, I know that "Stranger Things" is a popular Netflix show. That\'s given as a fact. Now, the next part says that if a Netflix show is popular, Karen will binge-watch it. So, since "Stranger Things" is popular, Karen must be binge-watching it. .\n\nThen, it says that Karen will download a show only if and only if she binge-watches it. The phrase "if and only if" is important here because it means it\'s a biconditional statement. So, Karen downloads a show exactly when she\'s binge-watching it. She doesn\'t download shows she doesn\'t binge-watch, and she doesn\'t binge-watch shows she doesn\'t download.\n\nNext, it mentions that Karen does not download "Black Mirror." So, since she doesn\'t download it, according to the previous statement, she also doesn\'t binge-watch it. .\n\nNow, it\'s given that "Black Mirror" is a Netflix show. So, if Karen doesn\'t binge-watch it, then it\'s not a popular show because the first statement says that only popular shows get binged by Karen. .\n\nWait, let me make sure I\'m not mixing anything up. The first statement is "If a Netflix show is popular, Karen will binge-watch it." That doesn\'t necessarily mean that all shows Karen binges are popular, but it\'s a one-directional implication. However, the second part is "If and only if Karen binge-watches a Netflix show, she will download it." So, that\'s a biconditional, meaning that downloading and binge-watching are equivalent for her. .\n\nSince Karen doesn\'t download "Black Mirror," she doesn\'t binge-watch it. Therefore, "Black Mirror" isn\'t a show she\'s binged on. But the first statement says that if a show is popular, she will binge it. But it doesn\'t say that if she binges it, it\'s popular. Wait, no, actually, the first statement is "If a Netflix show is popular, Karen will binge-watch it." That\'s a one-way implication. It doesn\'t say that all shows she binges are popular, only that popular shows are ones she binges.\n\nBut then, the second statement is "If and only if Karen binge-watches a Netflix show, she will download it." So, that means that her downloading a show is equivalent to her binging it. So, if she doesn\'t download it, she doesn\'t binge it. .\n\nBut the key point is that "Black Mirror" is a Netflix show, and she doesn\'t download it. So, she doesn\'t binge it either. But the first statement says that if a show is popular, she will binge it. So, does that mean that if she doesn\'t binge it, the show isn\'t popular? Not necessarily, because the first statement is an implication, not a biconditional. .\n\nWait, let me think again. The first statement is "If a Netflix show is popular, Karen will binge-watch it." So, popularity implies she binges it. But it doesn\'t say that she only binges popular shows. So, she might binge some other shows that aren\'t popular, but the context doesn\'t mention that. .\n\nHowever, the second statement is "If and only if Karen binge-watches a Netflix show, she will download it." So, that\'s a biconditional. So, she downloads a show exactly when she binges it. .\n\nGiven that she doesn\'t download "Black Mirror," she doesn\'t binge it. So, "Black Mirror" is a Netflix show, and she doesn\'t binge it. But the first statement says that if a show is popular, she will binge it. So, does that mean that if she doesn\'t binge it, it\'s not popular? .\n\nWait, no. The first statement is an implication: popularity → binging. The contrapositive would be: not binging → not popularity. So, if she doesn\'t binge it, then it\'s not a popular show. .\n\nBut she doesn\'t download "Black Mirror," so she doesn\'t binge it. Therefore, by contrapositive, "Black Mirror" is not popular. .\n\nWait, but the first statement is "If a Netflix show is popular, Karen will binge-watch it." So, popularity is a sufficient condition for her binging, but not necessarily a necessary condition. So, she might have other reasons to binge other shows that aren\'t popular. But in this case, since she doesn\'t download "Black Mirror," she doesn\'t binge it. .\n\nSo, using the contrapositive, if she doesn\'t binge it, then it\'s not popular. Therefore, "Black Mirror" is not popular. .\n\nSo, the statement "Black Mirror" is popular would be false</think>.\n\nThe statement "Black Mirror" is popular is false. .\n\nStep-by-step explanation:.\n1. "Stranger Things" is popular, so Karen binges it.\n2. Karen only downloads shows she binges, so she downloads "Stranger Things.".\n3. "Black Mirror" is a Netflix show, but Karen doesn\'t download it, so she doesn\'t binge it.\n4. Since Karen doesn\'t binge "Black Mirror," it\'s not a popular show (contrapositive of the first statement). Therefore, my final option is: ']] 64%|######4   | 131/204 [00:45<00:18,  3.90it/s]
