messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B or C.\n####\n# Context:\nAll mammals are animals. All monkeys are mammals. An animal is either a monkey or a bird. All birds fly. All animals breathe. If something can fly, then it has wings. If Rock is neither a fly nor a bird, then Rock neither flies nor breathes.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or uncertain? Rock does not have wings.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Uncertain', '<think>\nOkay, so I\'m trying to figure out whether the statement "Rock does not have wings" is true, false, or uncertain based on the given context. Let me break down the information step by step.\n\nFirst, the context tells me that all mammals are animals. Then, all monkeys are mammals, so monkeys are also animals. It also says that an animal is either a monkey or a bird. Wait, so does that mean that all animals are either monkeys or birds? Or is it that every animal is either a monkey or a bird? Hmm, I think it\'s the latter because it says "An animal is either a monkey or a bird." So, every animal falls into one of those two categories.\n\nNext, all birds fly, and all animals breathe. There\'s a rule that if something can fly, then it has wings. So, flying implies having wings. Now, the last statement is a bit tricky: "If Rock is neither a fly nor a bird, then Rock neither flies nor breathes." Let me parse that.\n\nSo, Rock is not a fly and Rock is not a bird. From the previous statements, since all animals are either monkeys or birds, and all birds fly, but Rock isn\'t a bird or a fly. Wait, but if Rock isn\'t a bird, and all animals are either monkeys or birds, then Rock can\'t be an animal because it\'s not a bird. So Rock isn\'t an animal. But the statement says if Rock isn\'t a fly or a bird, then it doesn\'t fly or breathe. Hmm.\n\nWait, let me think again. The statement is: If Rock is neither a fly nor a bird, then Rock neither flies nor breathes. So, Rock is not a fly and not a bird. From the earlier information, since all animals are either monkeys or birds, and all monkeys are mammals, which are animals, so Rock isn\'t an animal. But the statement is about Rock\'s ability to fly or breathe.\n\nSo, if Rock isn\'t a fly or a bird, then it doesn\'t fly or breathe. But wait, the context says all animals breathe, but Rock isn\'t an animal. So, does that mean Rock doesn\'t breathe? Or is it possible that Rock could still breathe even if it\'s not an animal?\n\nWait, the statement says if Rock is neither a fly nor a bird, then Rock neither flies nor breathes. So, Rock isn\'t a fly or a bird, so it doesn\'t fly and doesn\'t breathe. But does that mean Rock doesn\'t have wings? Because earlier, it\'s stated that if something can fly, it has wings. So, if Rock doesn\'t fly, does that mean it doesn\'t have wings? Or could it have wings without flying?\n\nWait, the rule is: If something can fly, then it has wings. So, the contrapositive would be: If something doesn\'t have wings, then it can\'t fly. But it doesn\'t say anything about things that have wings but can\'t fly. So, Rock not flying doesn\'t necessarily mean it doesn\'t have wings. It just means it can\'t fly.\n\nBut the statement we\'re evaluating is about whether Rock has wings. The question is whether Rock does not have wings. So, from the given information, can we conclude that Rock doesn\'t have wings?\n\nLet me see. Rock isn\'t a fly or a bird. Since all animals are either monkeys or birds, Rock isn\'t an animal. So, Rock isn\'t a mammal, not a bird, not a monkey, etc. But the statement about flying and wings only applies to things that can fly. Rock isn\'t a fly, so it doesn\'t have to have wings. Wait, no, the rule is that if something can fly, it has wings. So, Rock can\'t fly, but that doesn\'t mean it doesn\'t have wings. It just means it can\'t fly. So, Rock could have wings but not fly, or it could not have wings and not fly.\n\nBut the statement in the context says that if Rock is neither a fly nor a bird, then Rock neither flies nor breathes. So, Rock doesn\'t fly, and it doesn\'t breathe. But the question is about having wings. The fact that Rock doesn\'t fly doesn\'t necessarily mean it doesn\'t have wings. It just means it can\'t fly. So, Rock could have wings or not; the given information doesn\'t specify.\n\nWait, but the statement in the context says that if Rock is neither a fly nor a bird, then it neither flies nor breathes. So, Rock doesn\'t fly, and it doesn\'t breathe. But the question is about wings. The only thing we know is that if something can fly, it has wings. So, Rock can\'t fly, but that doesn\'t tell us anything about whether it has wings or not. It could have wings or not; the information doesn\'t specify.\n\nTherefore, the statement "Rock does not have wings" is uncertain because the given information doesn\'t provide enough evidence to conclude that Rock doesn\'t have wings. It only tells us that Rock doesn\'t fly, but having wings is independent of flying, except that flying requires wings. So, Rock could have wings or not, but we don\'t know for sure.\n\nWait, but the statement in the context says that if Rock is neither a fly nor a bird, then it neither flies nor breathes. So, Rock doesn\'t fly, and it doesn\'t breathe. But the question is about wings. The only thing we know is that if something can fly, it has wings. So, Rock can\'t fly, but it might have wings or not. The context doesn\'t say anything about non-animals having wings or not. So, we can\'t conclude anything about wings based on the given information.\n\nTherefore, the statement "Rock does not have wings" is uncertain because the information doesn\'t provide enough to confirm or deny that Rock has wings.\n</think>.\n\nThe statement "Rock does not have wings" is uncertain because the given information doesn\'t provide enough evidence to confirm or deny that Rock has wings. Therefore, my final option is: ']] 70%|######9   | 142/204 [00:50<00:13,  4.63it/s]
