messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B or C.\n####\n# Context:\nIf people work in student jobs on campus, then they need to earn money to help pay for their college tuition. If people order takeout frequently in college, then they work in student jobs on campus. People either order takeout frequently in college or enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes. If people enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes, then they are not picky eaters. If people enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes, then they spend a lot of their time eating and catching up with friends in the campus dining halls. If Mary works in student jobs on campus and needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition, then Mary is neither picky nor needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or uncertain? Mary is not a picky eater and spends a lot of her time eating and catching up with friends in the campus dining halls.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Uncertain', "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out whether the statement about Mary is true, false, or uncertain based on the given context. Let me break it down step by step.\n\nFirst, the context tells me that if people work in student jobs on campus, they need to earn money for college tuition. Mary works in student jobs, so she needs to earn money. That means she must be ordering takeout frequently because the context says that if people order takeout, they work in student jobs. So Mary orders takeout a lot.\n\nNow, the next part says that if people enjoy dining hall meals, they aren't picky eaters. But wait, Mary orders takeout, which implies she doesn't enjoy dining hall meals because if she did, she wouldn't need to order takeout. So Mary isn't picky, which aligns with the first part of the statement.\n\nThe context also mentions that if someone enjoys dining hall meals, they spend a lot of time eating and hanging out with friends. But since Mary isn't picky, she doesn't enjoy dining hall meals, so she doesn't spend a lot of time there. Instead, she's probably more into takeout and maybe socializing in the dining halls but not as much as someone who enjoys the meals.\n\nPutting it all together, the statement says Mary is not a picky eater and spends a lot of time eating and catching up with friends. Wait, but if she's not picky, she doesn't enjoy the dining hall meals, so she wouldn't be spending a lot of time there. Instead, she's probably more into takeout and maybe other activities. So the second part of the statement is actually false because she doesn't spend a lot of time eating in the dining halls.\n\nHmm, but I'm a bit confused because the context says that if you enjoy dining hall meals, you spend a lot of time there. But since Mary isn't picky, she doesn't enjoy them, so she doesn't spend that time. So the statement that she does spend a lot of time is incorrect. Therefore, the statement is false.\n</think>.\n\nThe statement is false because Mary doesn't enjoy dining hall meals, so she doesn't spend a lot of time there. Therefore, my final option is: "]]
