n.\n\nWait, but let me double-check. If John has a child, he can\'t have a wedding, so he can\'t have a well-attended one. If he doesn\'t have a child, he might have a wedding, but whether it\'s well-attended is unknown. So, it\'s possible that John has a well-attended wedding, making the statement false, or he doesn\'t, making it true. Therefore, it\'s uncertain.\n\nWait, but the question is asking if the statement "John does not have a well-attended wedding" is true, false, or uncertain. So, if it\'s possible that John does have a well-attended wedding, then the statement is not necessarily true. It could be true or false depending on the situation, so it\'s uncertain.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s another angle. Let\'s consider that if John has a well-attended wedding, then according to Premise 6, he must have traveled to Paris. But traveling to Paris implies he has a wedding, which is already the case. But Premise 4 says some weddings in July are well-attended, but it doesn\'t specify if John\'s is in July or not.\n\nWait, but if John\'s wedding is in July and well-attended, then he must have traveled to Paris. But if his wedding is not in July, he still must have traveled to Paris. So, in either case, he must have traveled to Paris, which implies he has a wedding, but that doesn\'t necessarily mean it\'s well-attended.\n\nBut the key is that Premise 4 only tells us that some in July are well-attended, not necessarily John\'s. So, we can\'t conclude anything about John specifically beyond what\'s already given.\n\nTherefore, the statement "John does not have a well-attended wedding" is uncertain because it\'s possible that John does have one, making the statement false, or he doesn\'t, making it true. There\'s no information that definitively says one way or the other.\n\nWait, but let me think again. If John has a child, he can\'t have a wedding, so he can\'t have a well-attended one. If he doesn\'t have a child, he might have a wedding, but whether it\'s well-attended is unknown. So, the statement could be true or false depending on whether he has a child or not. But the premises don\'t give us information about whether John has a child or not.\n\nWait, but Premise 5 says that if John has a child, then he doesn\'t have a wedding. But it doesn\'t say anything about John not having a child. So, John could have a child or not. If he has a child, he can\'t have a wedding. If he doesn\'t, he might have one.\n\nBut the question is about whether he has a well-attended wedding. So, if he has a child, he can\'t have a wedding, so the statement is true. If he doesn\'t have a child, he might have a well-attended wedding, making the statement false. Since we don\'t know if he has a child, we can\'t be certain.\n\nWait, but Premise 5 is a conditional: If John has a child, then he doesn\'t have a wedding. It doesn\'t say anything about the converse. So, John could have a child and not have a wedding, or he could not have a child and have a wedding or not.\n\nBut the question is about whether he has a well-attended wedding. So, if he has a child, he can\'t have a wedding, so he can\'t have a well-attended one. If he doesn\'t have a child, he might have a wedding, but whether it\'s well-attended is unknown.\n\nBut the question is whether the statement "John does not have a well-attended wedding" is true, false, or uncertain. So, if John has a child, the statement is true. If he doesn\'t have a child, it\'s possible that he does have a well-attended wedding, making the statement false. Since we don\'t know if he has a child, we can\'t be certain.\n\nWait, but Premise 5 says that if he has a child, he doesn\'t have a wedding. So, if he has a child, he can\'t have a well-attended wedding. But if he doesn\'t have a child, he might have a wedding, but we don\'t know if it\'s well-attended.\n\nSo, the statement "John does not have a well-attended wedding" could be true if he has a child, or it could be false if he doesn\'t have a child but