 does not have a wedding.\n\nSo, having a child leads to no wedding. But Premise 1 says that all weddings are for people with at least one child. So, if John has a wedding, he must have a child. But Premise 5 says that if he has a child, he can\'t have a wedding. So, this seems like a contradiction. Therefore, John cannot have a wedding because if he did, he would have a child (from Premise 1), but then Premise 5 says he can\'t have a wedding if he has a child. So, John cannot have a wedding at all.\n\nWait, but Premise 4 says some weddings in July are well-attended. So, if John can\'t have a wedding, then the statement "John has a wedding that is well-attended" would be false because he doesn\'t have a wedding.\n\nBut let me check again. Maybe I\'m missing something.\n\nIf John has a wedding, then he must have a child (Premise 1). But if he has a child, he can\'t have a wedding (Premise 5). So, John cannot have a wedding. Therefore, the statement "John has a wedding that is well-attended" is false because he doesn\'t have a wedding at all.\n\nWait, but Premise 6 says that if John has a well-attended wedding, then either he has a wedding in July or he travels to Paris. But since John can\'t have a wedding (from above), then the condition in Premise 6 is irrelevant because the antecedent is false. So, the statement "John has a well-attended wedding" would be false because he can\'t have a wedding.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I\'m overcomplicating. Let\'s see:.\n\nIf John has a wedding, then he must have a child (Premise 1). But if he has a child, he can\'t have a wedding (Premise 5). Therefore, John cannot have a wedding. So, the statement "John has a wedding that is well-attended" is false because he doesn\'t have a wedding</think>.\n\nThe statement "John has a wedding that is well-attended" is false because John cannot have a wedding at all. Having a wedding requires having a child (Premise 1), but having a child means John cannot have a wedding (Premise 5). Therefore,