e 2 says all dogs bark, if KiKi is a dog, then KiKi barks, so Jane doesn\'t like it. But I don\'t know if Jane\'s preferences affect the statement about KiKi, so maybe that\'s not directly relevant here.\n\nPremise 3: If an animal jumps, Jane likes it. Premise 4: If an animal has legs, then it jumps. Premise 5: If an animal is terricolous, then it has legs.\n\nNow, premise 6 is a bit more complex: If KiKi neither jumps nor has legs, then KiKi is terricolous or has legs. Let\'s parse this. The antecedent is "KiKi neither jumps nor has legs," which is equivalent to "KiKi does not jump and KiKi does not have legs." The consequent is "KiKi is terricolous or has legs," which is "KiKi is terricolous or KiKi has legs.".\n\nWait, that seems a bit confusing. Let me rephrase premise 6: If KiKi doesn\'t jump and doesn\'t have legs, then either KiKi is terricolous or has legs. But if KiKi doesn\'t have legs, then the "or legs" part is automatically true, regardless of whether it\'s terricolous or not. So, if KiKi doesn\'t jump and doesn\'t have legs, then the consequent is true because it has legs. So, the implication is that if KiKi doesn\'t jump and doesn\'t have legs, then it\'s either terricolous or has legs. But since it doesn\'t have legs, the consequent is still true because of the legs part. So, this seems a bit tautological, but maybe I\'m missing something.\n\nWait, perhaps I should approach this differently. Let\'s consider the statement we\'re evaluating: "KiKi neither barks nor is a dog." So, let\'s assume KiKi is a dog. Then, from premise 2, KiKi barks. But the statement says KiKi neither barks nor is a dog, so if KiKi is a dog, the statement is false. Therefore, if KiKi is a dog, the statement is false.\n\nAlternatively, if KiKi is not a dog, then we need to see if it\'s possible that KiKi doesn\'t bark. But premise 2 says all dogs bark, so if KiKi is a dog, it barks. If KiKi is not a dog, we don\'t know if it barks or not. So, KiKi could be a non-dog that doesn\