From the second premise, R → S. So, if he has access to a program, he can compose.\n\nNow, the third premise ties together can compose (S) and wants to compose (Q) to will compose (T). So, if both S and Q are true, then T is true.\n\nSo, if George likes music (P), then Q is true. If he has access to a program (R), then S is true. So, if both P and R are true, then both Q and S are true. Therefore, (S ∧ Q) is true, which by the third premise leads to T being true.\n\nTherefore, if P and R are true, then T must be true. So, the statement is true.\n\nWait, but let me make sure I\'m not missing anything. Is there any scenario where P and R are true, but T is false? Let\'s see.\n\nIf George likes music, he wants to compose (Q is true). If he has a program, he can compose (S is true). So, both Q and S are true. Then, according to the third premise, since both are true, he will compose (T is true). So, there\'s no way for P and R to be true without T being true.\n\nTherefore, the statement is necessarily true.\n</think>.\n\nThe statement is true because if George likes music and has access to a program, both conditions lead to him wanting and being able to compose, which results in him composing. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
